  "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032} & ={u}^{\u2032}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033} & ={u}^{\u2033}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{\u2033}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})-2({u}^{\u2032}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}-2{u}^{\u2032}{e}^{x}-2u{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}{e}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2033}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{\u2032}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{\u2032}=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{\u2032},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}={c}_{1}\\,\u27f9\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{2x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}.\n$$",
